From: Runeon Contributors <dev@runeon.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: Saturate a relative timer time too distant to store.

Windows takes any negative due time in NtSetTimer() as relative, so a
huge one such as -MAXLONGLONG means a time some 29,000 years away: the
timer does not fire. set_timer() and timeout_to_abstime() store a
relative time as expire - monotonic_time, which overflows a signed
64-bit value for such a due time, and the timer was signalled at once.
Due times of -MAXLONGLONG and of QueryPerformanceCounter() minus
MAXLONGLONG reproduce it on the previous patch set's runtime; -2^62 stays unsignalled.

The threadpool timer thread reaches this whenever threadpool timers
exist but none is relative: update_timers() arms its relative timer
with rel_now minus MAXLONGLONG, so that timer fires at once, wakes the
thread and is armed again the same way.

Clamp a relative time that cannot be stored to the most distant one
that can, a stored value of -MAXLONGLONG. Every relative time that
fits, every absolute time and TIMEOUT_INFINITE are unchanged.
The clamped value is negated and offset by monotonic_time in the
timeout list and in abstime_to_timeout() without overflowing; it is
never reached by the periodic re-arm, which only runs when the timer
fires. This covers every NtSetTimer() caller. No protocol change.

Another downstream project works around the same overflow by clamping
the due time in NtSetTimer(); no code is taken from it.

diff --git a/server/timer.c b/server/timer.c
--- a/server/timer.c
+++ b/server/timer.c
@@ -191,6 +191,9 @@
         timer->signaled = 0;
         reset_sync( timer->sync );
     }
+    /* Windows accepts any negative relative time; one too distant to be stored as a
+     * monotonic time must not wrap around, so give it the most distant one instead */
+    if (expire < monotonic_time - TIMEOUT_INFINITE) expire = monotonic_time - TIMEOUT_INFINITE;
     timer->when     = (expire <= 0) ? expire - monotonic_time : max( expire, current_time );
     timer->period   = period;
     timer->callback = callback;
